 OOP is not about inheritance. 
 It is about designing types that are hard to misuse. 
�� OOP Worksheet & Study Guide 
Making Your Own Types Feel Like the Language Meant Them to Exist Due: 16 Feb 2026 by class time. 
If this feels long, good. 
If it feels unfinished, also good. 
If you feel like thereʼs “always more,” congratulations — that feeling is the curriculum. This document serves three purposes: 
. Homework 
. Study guide 
. A mild personality test for how you think about software design 
Read the questions carefully. Many are intentionally phrased to trip up rote memorization. 
Part 0 — A Necessary Reality Check 
Most people think: 
OOP = inheritance 
Thatʼs like saying:
Math = long division 
Inheritance exists. 
It is sometimes useful. 
It is wildly overused. 
This worksheet is about building abstract data types (ADTs) that behave like they belong in the language — not about summoning class hierarchies like Pokémon. 
Part 1 — Review, But With Consequences 
 Stacks & Queues (ADT Reality Check) 
Answer clearly and concisely. 
. Compare array-based vs list-based implementations of stacks and queues: 
memory layout: contiguous memory, Nodes scattered (holds pointers)
resizing behavior: occasional, dynamic allocation
cache friendliness: excellent, Poor(scattered memory)
(Yes, cache friendliness matters. No, you may not ignore it.) 
. Why is std::vector a natural fit for a stack, but awkward for a queue? 
Supports push_back and pop_back. 
Requires pop_front, which makes things awkward.
. Define the invariant for: 
a stack: only top element is accessible; push/pop affect the top
a queue: Only front element can be removed and only the back element can be added. 
If your invariant takes more than one sentence, itʼs not an invariant — itʼs a confession. Part 2 — Overloading vs Overriding 
(Same Word Root, Completely Different Beasts) 
 Conceptual Distinction 
Fill out the table: 
Feature Overloading Overriding 
Resolved at: Compile Time,  Run Time. 
Requires inheritance?  No, Yes  
Same function name? Yes, yes  
Same parameter list? No, No
Polymorphism involved? Compile Time Poly, Run time Poly 
Then answer: 
. Why is overloading a compile-time convenience? Static Binding 
. Why is overriding a runtime contract? Dynamic Binding
. Why do beginners confuse the two? Involves methods with the same name, use similar syntax, and often appear in the same class hierarchy. 
. Why is that confusion dangerous? Unexpected behavior and logic errors. 
Hint: The compiler is not your therapist. It will not guess your intent.
 Part 3 — Constructors & Initialization Lists 
(Where C++ Stops Holding Your Hand) 
 Initialization Lists or Else 
Given: 
class Widget { 
private: 
 const int id; 
 std::string name; 
public: 
 Widget(int id, std::string name); 
}; 
Answer: 
. Why must this constructor use an initialization list? Id is const.
. What happens if you try to assign id inside the constructor body? Error. Const members are immutable 
-  Write the correct constructor. : id(id), name(name) {}
. Name one other situation where initialization lists are required (research-lite). Reference members 
Saying “because the compiler told me to” is not an explanation. 
 Copy Constructor vs Assignment Operator 
Research + reasoning required. 
. When is the copy constructor invoked? The copy constructor is called when a new object is being created as a copy of another object
. When is the assignment operator invoked? The assignment operator is called when an existing object is assigned the value of another existing object
. Why do both exist? Copy constructor: Ensures proper initialization of a new object from another, handling deep copies if necessary.


Assignment operator: Ensures proper copy of data from one existing object to another, possibly releasing old resources first.

. What subtle bugs appear if you confuse them? Double deletion / memory leaks, Dangling pointers, Resource corruption

“They both copy stuff” earns partial credit and a sigh. 
Part 4 — struct vs class 
(Same Machine Code, Different Intent) 
 Design Signal, Not Syntax Sugar 
Answer: 
. What is the only language-level difference between struct and class? Struct:  members and base classes are public by default


Class: members and base classes are private by default
.
Why does C++ even allow both? Historical / readability reasons
. When does choosing struct communicate intent better than class? If a type is primarily data with public access and minimal behavior, struct communicates that clearly 
. Why does intent matter more than syntax in large systems? Large codebases rely on human readability and maintainability. Choosing struct vs class communicates how a type should be used
Part 5 — Operator Overloading 
(Where ADTs Start Feeling Real) 
 Rules You Donʼt Get to Ignore 
Research and explain: 
. Why canʼt C++ overload: 
. 
:: 
Overloading (.) and (::)  would break type safety and make the compiler unable to find members reliably.
sizeof 
Overloading sizeof would require runtime behavior for something that is inherently compile-time
. Why should operator+ not mutate the left-hand operand? ‘+’ is arithmetic, not assignment. Mutating the left-hand operand would violate user expectations and break referential transparency. 
. Why is operator<< almost never a member function? usually ostream; must be a free function to support natural syntax
If your answer is “because thatʼs how everyone does it,” dig deeper. 
 The “other / rhs” Trap (Yes, Itʼs Intentional) 
Given: 
Point operator+(const Point& rhs) const; 
Answer clearly: 
. Which object owns this function? The function is a member of the class Point, so it belongs to the object on the left-hand side (the one calling it).
. What does rhs represent? (right-hand side) is the argument passed to the operator
. How can this function access rhs.x if x is private? the operator+ function can still read it
. What does this tell you about class-level vs object-level access? 
Class-level access: Private/protected members are accessible to all instances of the same class, not just the current object (this).


Object-level access is a misleading mental model — private does not mean “only this object”, it means “only members/functions of this class”.

This question exists specifically to break incorrect mental models. 
Part 6 — friend: Controlled Violation of Privacy 
 Friend or Design Smell? 
Answer: 
. What does the friend keyword actually do? Allows access to private and protected data
. Why is operator<< commonly declared as a friend? Its clean syntax
. Why is excessive use of friend a red flag? Breaks encapsulation 
. Give one legitimate use case and one illegitimate one. 
“Because it wouldnʼt compile otherwise” is not a justification — itʼs a symptom. Legitimate: Non-member operator like operator<< or operator== needing access to private members for efficiency or syntatic clarity. 
Illegitimate: Making a random utility function a friend to bypass instead of providing proper getter/setter.
 Part 7 — Core Programming Task 
Design a Type, Not a School Assignment 
 Build a Native-Feeling Point2D 
You are designing a type, not checking boxes. 
Required Features 
Your Point2D class must: 
Use private data members 
Include at least three constructors: 
default 
parameterized 
copy constructor 
Use initialization lists 
Overload: 
+ 
- 
== 
!= (without duplicating logic) 
<< 
Demonstrate const correctness 
Avoid public getters unless you can justify them 
Required Usage (This Must Compile)
Point2D a(3, 4); 
Point2D b(1, 2); 
Point2D c = a + b; 
Point2D d = a - b; 
if (c == Point2D(4, 6)) { 
 std::cout << "Math still works.\n"; 
} 
std::cout << a << std::endl; 

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Point2D {
private:
    int x;
    int y;
public:
    //Default
    Point2D() : x(0), y(0) {}
    //Parameterized
    Point2D(int xVal, int yVal) : x(xVal), y(yVal) {}
    //Copy
    Point2D(const Point2D& other) : x(other.x), y(other.y) {}
	//Addition
    Point2D operator+(const Point2D& rhs) const {
        return Point2D(x + rhs.x, y + rhs.y);
    }
	//Subtraction
    Point2D operator-(const Point2D& rhs) const {
        return Point2D(x - rhs.x, y - rhs.y);
    }
	Equality
    bool operator==(const Point2D& rhs) const {
        return x == rhs.x && y == rhs.y;
    }
	//No duplicated logic
    bool operator!=(const Point2D& rhs) const {
        return !(*this == rhs);
    }
	//Output
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Point2D& p) {
        os << "(" << p.x << ", " << p.y << ")";
        return os;
    }
};
int main() {
    Point2D a(3, 4);
    Point2D b(1, 2);

    Point2D c = a + b;
    Point2D d = a - b;

    if (c == Point2D(4, 6)) {
        cout << "Math still works.\n";
    }
    cout << a << endl;
    cout << d << endl;
    return 0;
}

 Design Constraints (Read Carefully) 
You may not: 
expose raw data publicly 
use inheritance 
overload operators with nonsense semantics 
You should: 
minimize the public interface 
make misuse difficult 
prefer clarity over cleverness 


Part 8 — Composition (The Quiet MVP of OOP) 
 Composition in Plain English 
Composition means assembling behavior from parts, not becoming those parts. Or more bluntly: 
“Has-a” beats “is-a” most of the time. 
 Inheritance vs Composition (No Dragons Yet) 
❌ Inheritance First Instinct 
class ColoredPoint : public Point2D { 
 Color color; 
};
Problems: 
Locked into Point2D forever 
Inherits everything, wanted or not 
Hard to evolve safely 
✅ Composition Approach 
class ColoredPoint { 
private: 
 Point2D position; 
 Color color; 
}; 
Benefits: 
Clear ownership 
Modular behavior 
Fewer unintended side effects 
 The Big Takeaway 
Inheritance expresses identity. 
Composition expresses capability. 
Most real systems care more about capability. 
Part 9 — Reflection (Yes, This Is Graded) 
Answer honestly: 
. Does your Point2D feel like a built-in type? Yes 
. What design choice most contributed to that feeling? The value semantics
. Which OOP concept currently feels overhyped? I honestly don’t feel like any of it seems too overhyped at the moment. 
. Which one feels underrated? Friend operator, it is a long code that is very important to have memorized. 
. What part of this worksheet made you uncomfortable — and why? I would say overloading vs overriding, just because it took me a while to truly understand the slight differences between the two. Hoping I don’t mess them up on the exam. 
